  "notes": "'Adapted for a frontage of 30 feet, and would be conveniently placed at the angle of a road, which position is always preferable on account of its more commanding position, the extra light and prospect gained on one side, and the means afforded of communication at the back of the house.' Three floors, the attic repeating the first. 'The elevations are of an Italian character, and the dormer windows lighting the Attics are converted into ornamental features giving a picturesque effect to the roof.' Storey heights are given and are not recorded as room dimensions: ground 10'6, first 9'0, attic 7'0, the ground floor raised 2'3 above the ground level. The plate confirms the three ground-floor dimensions and draws the entrance passage, hall and back passage the specification describes, with closets either side of the kitchen and the coal cellar and pantry off the back passage. The elevation is Italian with a mansard and three ornamental dormers - which is what 'the dormer windows lighting the Attics are converted into ornamental features' means.",
